course: dictionary
lesson: 1496
title: Sound words: caw
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words copy the sound they mean. Say caw out loud and it sounds like the harsh cry of a crow. Context helps you find the meaning: «every day with a crow cawing and every day without» tells you that cawing is something a crow does.
explain: Caw is a sound word, so its meaning is in what you hear. A crow's cry is loud and harsh, not sweet like a song. Crows, rooks and ravens all make this noise, so the word tells you a dark bird is nearby.
explain: Caw can be a verb or a noun. As a verb, crows caw from the trees. As a noun, you hear a caw. In «cawing crows», the word cawing describes the crows as they make that sound.
fact: Caw is a sound word: it copies the sound it means.
fact: «Caw» means the loud, harsh cry of a crow, rook or raven.
fact: As a verb, caw means to make this sound. As a noun, a caw is the cry itself.
fact: The verb forms are caws, cawed and cawing.
fact: Writers use «caw» to set a spooky or wild scene.
exercise: «Every day with a crow cawing and every day without.» What does caw mean? | *The loud, harsh cry of a crow | A soft breeze in the trees | A quiet tap on a door | A sweet song from a robin
exercise: Find and copy the word that tells you the crows are making the sound. | *cawing | lucky | without | socks
exercise: What is the past tense of caw? | Cawing | Caws | Calling | *Cawed
test: In «the crows cawed at dawn», what does cawed mean? | Sang sweetly | *Made a loud, harsh cry | Slept quietly | Ran fast
test: Is caw a verb, a noun or both? | Only a noun | Only a name | Only an adjective | *Both: it can be a verb and a noun
exam: Which sentence uses caw as a sound word for a crow? | The crow ate a worm | *Crows caw loudly from the trees | Crows fly south in winter | The sky was grey
